1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,000
Welcome back. By now you know Claude can call tools, and you've seen MCP servers and skills come up along the way.

2
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:13,000
There are four different ways to give Claude a capability, and they don't all do the same job.

3
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:17,000
This lesson walks through each one and what makes it distinct.

4
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:21,000
Let's put the four options on the table before we go deep on any of them.

5
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:27,000
First, built-in tools. These are ready-made capabilities Anthropic hosts for you, like web search

6
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:28,000
and code execution.

7
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:32,000
And you reach for them first when they cover the need.

8
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:38,000
Second, custom tools. These are functions you define and run yourself, for an action specific to

9
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:39,000
your own application.

10
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:46,000
Third, skills. A skill packages reusable instructions and workflow in a file, and Claude loads it

11
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:48,000
only when the task matches.

12
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:56,000
Fourth, MCP servers. These expose tools, resources, and prompts over a standard interface, so one

13
00:00:56,000 --> 00:00:58,000
integration works across many clients.

14
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:05,000
Four mechanisms. Four distinct fits. Hold that map, and the rest fills in.

15
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:11,000
Let's start with built-in tools, sometimes called server tools. The defining trait is where they run.

16
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:17,000
They execute on Anthropic's own infrastructure, so you write no-handler code, and the results come

17
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:18,000
straight back in the response.

18
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:24,000
Web search, web fetch, code execution, and tool search are the ones to know.

19
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:30,000
Because they're hosted for you, they're the fastest thing to adopt. You turn them on, and they work.

20
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:35,000
Here's the boundary that matters, though. A built-in tool runs on Anthropic Infra, which means it

21
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:36,000
can't reach your private systems.

22
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:43,000
It has no path to your internal database or your company's API. When you need that reach, a built-in

23
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:47,000
tool is the wrong instrument, and you'll want one of the next three.

24
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:54,000
Custom tools are the answer when you need Claude to do something specific to your application.

25
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:59,000
You define the tool yourself. You write the name, the description, and the input schema, and

26
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:02,000
critically, you execute the call in your own code.

27
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:09,000
That last part is the whole point. Because the tool runs in your app, it can reach anything your app can reach.

28
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:14,000
Your internal API, your database, a private service behind your firewall.

29
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:20,000
This is how you connect Claude to arbitrary internal systems that Anthropic could never host for you.

30
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:27,000
Claude returns a tool use request, your code runs the real work, and you hand back the result.

31
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:34,000
A custom tool is your general purpose way to give Claude one precise, app-specific action.

32
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:39,000
Let's put those two side-by-side, because the contrast is the clearest one here.

33
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:45,000
A built-in tool runs on Anthropic's infrastructure. You do no work to execute it, but it can only do

34
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:48,000
what Anthropic hosts, and it can't touch your private data.

35
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:54,000
A custom tool runs in your application. You do the work of writing and executing it.

36
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:58,000
And in exchange, it can reach anything your code can reach.

37
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:01,000
So the trade-off is convenience versus reach.

38
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:05,000
Built-in gives you zero effort capability inside a fixed set.

39
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:10,000
Custom gives you unlimited reach at the cost of writing and running the handler yourself.

40
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:13,000
When a built-in covers the need, take it.

41
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:17,000
When it can't reach far enough, you write a custom tool.

42
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:21,000
Now skills, which are a different kind of thing entirely.

43
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:25,000
A skill isn't a connection to an external system. It's packaged know-how.

44
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:31,000
Concretely, a skill is a folder with a file called skill.md at its root.

45
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:36,000
That file carries a little front matter, a name, and a description, followed by the instructions

46
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:40,000
themselves, and it can bundle scripts and other resources alongside.

47
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:44,000
The description is what makes it efficient.

48
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:50,000
Claude reads just that short description on each turn and loads the full skill, only when the

49
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:52,000
current task actually matches.

50
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:55,000
That's progressive disclosure.

51
00:03:55,000 --> 00:04:01,000
Because the heavy content stays out of context until it's relevant, the material you can pack into a

52
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:03,000
skill is effectively unbounded.

53
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:09,000
Think of a skill as teaching Claude a procedure, not wiring it to a system.

54
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:12,000
Let's make the role of a skill concrete.

55
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:16,000
A skill captures a workflow you want done the same way every time.

56
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:23,000
Say your team has a specific process for filing a report, with steps, formatting rules, and conventions.

57
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:29,000
You could re-explain it in every prompt or package it once as a skill and let Claude pull it in

58
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:30,000
whenever the task comes up.

59
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:37,000
The value is consistency and reuse of procedural knowledge, not connectivity.

60
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:41,000
One caveat worth knowing on the Claude API.

61
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:48,000
A skill can only use packages that are already installed, and it can't make its own external network calls.

62
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:56,000
So a skill that needs live outside data pairs with a tool or an MCP server that provides the connection.

63
00:04:56,000 --> 00:04:59,000
The skill supplies the how.

64
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:03,000
That brings us to MCP, the Model Context Protocol.

65
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:09,000
The first thing to get right is that MCP isn't a model and not a single API endpoint.

66
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:16,000
It's an open standard, a shared way for applications to connect to external tools and data.

67
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:23,000
People describe it as a USB-C port for Claude, one common connector instead of a custom integration for every system.

68
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:30,000
A single MCP server packages tools, resources, and prompts behind that standard interface, and any

69
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:32,000
compliant client can speak to it.

70
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:34,000
That's the payoff.

71
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:40,000
You build the integration once, and it's reusable across different hosts, teams, and applications,

72
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:42,000
rather than rewired for each app.

73
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:50,000
So where a custom tool is bespoke to one application, an MCP server is the standardized, shareable

74
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:51,000
way to expose a capability.

75
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:58,000
Before we recap, let me name the three dimensions that separate these mechanisms, because they set

76
00:05:58,000 --> 00:05:59,000
up how you choose.

77
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:09,000
First, reach. Can it touch your private systems? Built-in tools can't. Custom tools and MCP servers can.

78
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:15,000
Second, reuse scope. Is this for one application or shared across many?

79
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:23,000
A custom tool lives in one app, while an MCP server is built to be reused across clients and teams.

80
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:29,000
Third, context cost. How much does it consume before the conversation starts?

81
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:37,000
Loaded tool definitions take up space, whereas a skills progressive disclosure keeps its bulk out of context until needed.

82
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:45,000
Reach, reuse, and context keep those three straight, and choosing the right one becomes far more mechanical.

83
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:53,000
Let's recap. Four ways to extend Cloud. Built-in tools are hosted but limited. Custom tools reach your systems.

84
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:59,000
Skills package workflows, and MCP standardizes as shared integration.

85
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:04,000
Remember the three dimensions that separate them. Reach into your private systems, reuse scope

86
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:07,000
across applications, and the context cost each one carries.

87
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:14,000
That covers built-in tools, custom tools, skills, and MCPs. I'll see you in the next one.

